            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>AIR SEALING



Overall, your house is moderately leaky, and I have identified a number of locations that can be addressed:



** The largest source is the wide open combustion air pipe (typically 6" in diameter) located beside the furnace on the floor (appearing to be a heating duct, but it is not) - this pipe is likely a significant cause of the main floor being cold. With a furnace upgrade to a new condensing furnace (propane or oil) it will eliminate the combustion air pipe.



** The second largest air leakage source identified is around the older, slider windows, and likely around the window frames as well. Cold air could be felt coming in during the blower door test at many of the windows. If you are doing a window upgrade, be sure to upgrade to hinged style windows, and apply spray foam around the frames/ rough opening.



** Around the attic hatch (can seal with removable caulking such as "Draft Attack").



** The bathroom exhaust fan on the main floor. These can be replaced with energy star rated fans with butterfly style dampers.



** Around the crawl space hatch. The problem can be solved by applying weather stripping to the hatch



** Around the pellet stove flue penetration through the wall. Caulking can be applied here.



** Around the outside edge of the main floor exterior door. Consider replacing weather stripping as needed.



Savings: If you address the air leakage locations identified above, I estimate a modest 10% reduction in air leakage heat loss, but it could result in a larger reduction. This is estimated to save only a small amount of money per year - approximately $80 per year in energy costs and decrease your annual energy usage by 3.4 GJ. Although the dollar savings aren't huge, the increase in comfort for occupants will be noticeable, for relatively low effort/ expense.



There are currently no rebates available from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program for performing air sealing, however you will enjoy the annual energy savings every year.



CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant between $550 and $1000 is available for improving the airtightness of your home based on the level achieved. This is one of the most cost-effective energy-saving measures you can undertake.



An air-sealing grant is available if the airtightness of your home is improved to achieve or exceed the air change rate target proposed above. Achieving any of the targets defined for your home typically requires that the work be performed by an air-sealing professional.</airSeal>
                <windows>

WINDOWS



The windows in your house are a mixture of new windows, and old windows. Most windows are older triple paned, wood framed windows, but there are some newer vinyl framed windows with a low E coating. 



Upgrading specific windows in this house to ENERGY STAR?????? qualified windows rated for climate zone 3 is something that could be considered as an upgrade for this home. If this is something the homeowner wishes to pursue I recommend full frame replacement windows to vinyl frames that are triple pane, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged windows, with a U-Value (rate of heat loss) no higher than 0.25 BTU per hour.



Savings: By replacing all the older windows with ones that meet the criteria listed above, you could potentially save $80 per year in energy costs and decrease your annual energy usage by 3.3 GJ. 



There are currently no rebates available for installing new windows from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program, however there are rebates available from the Greener Homes Grant - Government of Canada. Please see their website for more details on program requirements.



CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, grants of either $125 or $250 per window rough opening are available when an ENERGY STAR certified or ENERGY STAR certified Most Efficient window is installed to replace an existing window. Replacing old, damaged or leaky windows with new ENERGY STAR certified products can help you save on energy, improve comfort and reduce noise. To determine eligibility of the product you are seeking to purchase, confirm that it is included on the list of eligible equipment as found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant webpage.







</windows>
                <doors>DOOR UPGRADE



The doors in your home are of various ages and styles, and considered to be in good condition, and the savings on new doors will be minimal. Weather stripping was noted to be in reasonable shape at all locations. There are currenlty no rebates available from the GNWT for door replacements.



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant of $125 per hinged door system is available when an eligible ENERGY STAR certified door or door system is installed. Replacing old, damaged or leaky hinged doors with new ENERGY STAR certified products can help you save on energy and improve comfort. To determine eligibility of the product you are seeking to purchase, confirm that it is included on the list of eligible equipment as found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant webpage.



--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------</doors>
                <HotWaterSystem>

PROPANE Instantaneous Upgrade from Electric



You currently have a 182 litre electric hot water tank. Although electric hot water tanks are efficient from the perspective that 100% of the electricity used either heats the water inside or the heat radiating from the tank supplements the heating of the home, electricity is very costly. You may want to consider replacing your electric hot water heater with an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with an energy factor of 0.92 or higher.



Savings: By replacing your current hot water tank with an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with a thermal efficiency of 0.92, you could potentially save approximately $1200 per year in energy costs, and qualify for up to $750 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



NOTE: The GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program is regularly reviewed and the rebate amount and/or eligibility criteria can change. In addition, rebate funding is provided on a first-come, first-serve basis contingent upon available funding. Please check our website before purchasing to determine current rebate amounts, eligibility criteria and if rebate funding is still available.</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>FURNACE UPGRADE



One of the largest sources of cold air and discomfort found in your home was the combustion air supply pipe that is open, and constantly dumping cold, outside air into your mechanical room during the heating season. This is a typical installation style for older furnaces, but by upgrading to a direct vent furnace, the combustion air, never interacts with air in the home. It eliminates that cold air source.



PROPANE FURNACE



Your house is currently heated with an older 82% efficient oil furnace. I have modeled the following furnace upgrade option for you:



An ENERGY STAR?????? qualified high-efficiency condensing propane furnace with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE, including a high efficiency ECM motor to circulate the heat.



- This upgrade is currently eligible for a $1,500 rebate from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program (EEIP). Full program details are outlined on our website: http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program.



This upgrade will eliminate the need for the current chimney and the 6" open combustion air supply pipe in the mechanical room. 



Savings:



By replacing your current propane furnace with a 95% AFUE condensing propane furnace that incorporates an ECM fan blower, you could potentially save $720 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 27 GJ, and qualify for up to $1,500 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program (EEIP). Please be aware that the modelling is based on using your furnace to supply 100% of your heating needs, which as was mentioned to me during the evaluation, that is not how your home is primarily heated. See below for more information about supplementary heating.



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



NOTE: The GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program is regularly reviewed and the rebate amount and/or eligibility criteria can change. In addition, rebate funding is provided on a first-come, first-serve basis contingent upon available funding. Please check our website before purchasing to determine current rebate amounts, eligibility criteria and if rebate funding is still available.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



SUPPLEMENTARY HEATING - Pellet Stove



You provided information during the evaluation that said you primarily use your pellet stove to heat your entire home. For modelling purposes I was able to model your home using your pellet stove to provide 75% of your heating requirements. The modelling showed that by using your current pellet stove as your primary heat source you are saving approximately $380 per year in heating costs, depending on the price of heating oil. The savings shown here may seem lower than anticipated, but it is important to remember that the efficiency of the heating appliance plays a big role in cost savings. A lower efficient appliance send more heat up the flue as waste, as opposed to into your home. This means that upgrading to a new, efficient, condensing propane furnace will save you more in heating costs than using the pellet stove as your primary heat source.



Another thing you can do to more evenly distribute the heat from your pellet stove (which is intended as a space heater) is to use the furnace fan to circulate the heat. In an older furnace like yours, this will add to your electricity bill, but if you were to upgrade the fan to an ECM fan, or upgrade to a new furnace including an ECM fan, the heat distribution cost would be minimal.



PELLET STOVE UPGRADE



You also mentioned that it was your intention to upgrade the pellet stove to a newer stove in the near future. I calculated the savings of going from your current pellet stove (efficiency of 73%) to a newer pellet stove ( 77% efficiency) and determined you will save an additional $60 annually by upgrading. The savings don't justify the upgrade in this case, however, if your current pellet stove is reaching the end of it's useful life then it's something to consider.



----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

</HeatingSystem>
                <AdditionalComments>

ADDITIONAL COMMENTS



LED Lights ????????? Hydro Community



Savings: Light Emitting Diode (LED) lights save about 80% compared to regular incandescent lighting, and about 20% compared to fluorescent lighting. Fluorescent lighting also contains mercury which is a hazardous waste material that requires special disposal. And LED lights work well in the cold and can last a long time.



Rebates: In Yellowknife, LED light bulbs, tubes and integrated fixtures are eligible for a rebate of 50% of the purchase price up to $ 200 (per fiscal year) from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Depressurization Test = Pass



The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment (kitchen range hood, bathroom fans and the clothes dryer), indicated a pressure drop of 1.1 Pascals. As this number is less than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment is not expected to cause combustion products to be drawn into your home, if you have any chimney vented appliances in your home that are not direct-vent. Your current oil furnace is susceptible to back drafting.



During my assessment of your home, I did see both smoke and carbon monoxide (CO) detectors present. Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed &amp; operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.



----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Ventilation



Your home currently has the following mechanical ventilation that vents to the outside of the house:

         

        - two bathroom fans

        - a clothes dryer



You may want to consider installing ENERGY STAR rated bathroom fans that seal well, use little electricity &amp; operate at low noise levels. Some newer bathroom fans have a built-in humidity sensor. Many homes now have timer switches connected to bathroom fans (where you select 5, 10, 15, 20 minutes) so these fans can remain on for an adequate time to remove bathroom moisture and/or odors without the risk of being left on all day and wasting heat.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Fuel Costs ????????? Yellowknife



The following Nov. 1, 2020 values, including GST, have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Yellowknife:



Electricity - $19.04 min, $0.33 per kWh

Oil - $0.91 per liter 

Propane - $0.61 per liter delivered

Wood - $420 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested

Pellets - $5.67 per bag, $353.85 per tonne bulk delivered



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Disclaimer



The foregoing report is provided as an assessment of the visible components only and shall not be taken as a guarantee or an acceptance of liability for future performance of any part of the building. This report is prepared with reasonable skill and care within the limitations of a visual inspection on the inspection date. This evaluation and report do not cover code compliance issues set by the authority having jurisdiction.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Your Energy Advisor



Please do not hesitate to contact me if you have any questions.



Sincerely,



Scott Dowler



Energy Management Specialist

Arctic Energy Alliance

Phone: (867) 920-3333

Fax: (867) 873-0303

E-mail: scott.dowler@aea.nt.ca



Arctic Energy Alliance: Taking action on high heating and power bills, high transportation costs, and climate change!

</AdditionalComments>
            </RURComments>
